<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Deal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The New Deal was the series of programmes, reforms and public works launched by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> between 1933 and 1939 in response to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>. It regulated banking and the stock market, created relief and employment schemes, supported farmers and labour unions and introduced Social Security, greatly expanding the role of the federal government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-deal.jpg" alt="New Deal" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LordHarris at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Deal_collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Great Depression that followed the crash of 1929 left about a quarter of the workforce unemployed and brought the <b>New Deal</b> of Franklin D. Roosevelt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1933 the federal Livestock Reduction cut the herds that gave the Navajo half their cash income, hurting women most, and left lasting opposition to John Collier’s Indian <b>New Deal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/navajo">Navajo · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The presidency grew with the <b>New Deal</b>, two world wars and the Cold War, until the historian Arthur Schlesinger Jr. warned in 1973 of an “imperial presidency”.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States Theodore Roosevelt held that wise progressivism and wise conservatism go hand in hand, and the word conservative was first used in its modern sense for pro-free enterprise opponents of the <b>New Deal</b>; the Republican Party became the country’s major conservative party.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States Franklin D. Roosevelt’s <b>New Deal</b> and Lyndon B. Johnson’s Great Society, with Medicare, Medicaid and the Civil Rights Act of 1964, expanded the welfare state.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Similar policies followed in France, in Britain with the Liberal welfare reforms, and in the United States in the Progressive Era and Franklin D. Roosevelt’s <b>New Deal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-democracy">Social democracy · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
